#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e84d5ab0-cec4-5988-9db3-8e296590d279" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integral Involving Other Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0945e89d-40a1-5bbd-947b-87ccb466c405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\ln(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317074} {:answer/id #uuid "d06b5910-98c0-53be-a28f-dad13df40442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0945e89d-40a1-5bbd-947b-87ccb466c405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\ln(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317074} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa485675-6e54-5354-a5be-95b88c6790a9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0aeedd54-c10a-53bd-b367-88f50ec08852" :question/math-academy-id "q-351320" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{3\\cdot {4}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[3\\cdot {4}^{x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(3-3\\cdot {4}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot (3-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }3\\cdot {4}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={4}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{4}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{4}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056962/assets/fd4020291b348d130fc34969d72ff683aab5b6fb95ea5f7d32b90e5245d56d9c.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot (3-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }3\\cdot {4}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334589 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc1fbb99-83e1-52ba-83f1-367de07351a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "427f5e75-f008-5509-bb6f-c1b5a13aa4f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\ln(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "427f5e75-f008-5509-bb6f-c1b5a13aa4f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\ln(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334591} :answer-field/id #uuid "454dc733-daaa-538f-850b-a61b7bf3568f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13cadb7c-3bc6-5457-8b82-42f904d7adf6" :question/math-academy-id "q-351511" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{-2\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{-2\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{6}^{-2u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{-2\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{6}^{-2u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{6}^{-2u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{6}^{-2u}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{6}^{-2u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{6}^{2x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2x}}=0$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14056962/assets/8f732515dd376db40889706419781e02cbedfcd4da8513a09e4f2d77ff88ece2.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{6}^{-2\\sqrt{x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{6}^{2a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
